      "id": 1,
      "text": "Yale was founded in 1890 by the Western Coal and Mining Company as a mining camp for African American workers.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        2,
        1
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1",
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://kids.kiddle.co/Yale,_Kansas",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "The town sat on the Missouri Pacific Railroad, which ferried black miners during the 1893 and 1899 strikes.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        3,
        4
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Yale%2C_Kansas",
      "category": "transport",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "In 1900, a group called the “Big Colored Band from Yale” entertained President Theodore Roosevelt during his visit to Pittsburg.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        5,
        9
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia",
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Yale%2C_Kansas",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "Yale’s post office opened in 1892 and closed in 1914, marking the town’s brief period of official postal service.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        20
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Yale%2C_Kansas",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "The community still hosts the historic Chicken Mary's and Chicken Annie's restaurants, echoing its early 20th‑century culinary scene.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        12
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
